.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt PSTORE 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pstore
.Nd crash records kept across a reset, under /sys/fs/pstore
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cm ramoops.mem_address Ns = Ns Ar ADDRESS
.Cm ramoops.mem_size Ns = Ns Ar BYTES
.Op Cm ramoops.record_size Ns = Ns Ar BYTES
.Op Cm ramoops.console_size Ns = Ns Ar BYTES
.Op Cm ramoops.ftrace_size Ns = Ns Ar BYTES
.Op Cm ramoops.pmsg_size Ns = Ns Ar BYTES
.Op Cm ramoops.mem_type Ns = Ns Ar N
.Op Cm ramoops.ecc Ns = Ns Ar N
.Sh DESCRIPTION
With the
.Cm ramoops.
options on the kernel's command line, the kernel keeps a region of RAM
that survives a warm reset, as Linux's ramoops does, and writes into it
what the next boot should see: the end of the log when the kernel panics,
and the log as the console prints it.
The next boot finds what the region holds and shows each record as a
file under
.Pa /sys/fs/pstore ,
which it mounts with
.Ql nosuid ,
.Ql nodev
and
.Ql noexec ,
as systemd mounts pstore on Linux.
Without the options there is no region and no
.Pa /sys/fs/pstore .
Nothing is written to the firmware's variables.
.Pp
The options are Linux's module parameters, read as Linux reads them;
each number may be decimal, octal with a leading 0, or hexadecimal with
a leading 0x:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm ramoops.mem_address , Cm ramoops.mem_size
The region's physical address and size, which must be under 4 GiB.
The kernel takes the region out of its memory before it gives any of it
out, as Linux's
.Cm memmap=
would, the whole of each page the region touches.
A region that is partly usable memory, or that holds the kernel or a
boot module, is refused with a record of why; one in memory the firmware
does not call usable is mapped and used as it is.
.It Cm ramoops.record_size
The size of each crash record, 4096 when not given: the region, less the
console, ftrace and pmsg zones, is cut into as many dmesg zones of at
least this size as fit, each of the same even size.
.It Cm ramoops.console_size
The size of the console zone, 4096 when not given; 0 for none.
.It Cm ramoops.ftrace_size , Cm ramoops.pmsg_size
Each 4096 when not given.
These zones keep their place after the console zone, as Linux lays them
out, so that both kernels find the same zones at the same options; this
kernel writes nothing to them.
.It Cm ramoops.mem_type
How the region is mapped: 0, the default, write-combining, with the
write buffers drained after each write; 1 uncached; 2 cached.
.It Cm ramoops.ecc
The Reed-Solomon parity bytes of each 128-byte block, and of each zone's
header: 0, the default, for none, 1 for 16, or 2 to 127.
A larger value is refused, since a 128-byte block and its parity must fit
in the code's 255 symbols; Linux does not handle one either.
The code is Linux's for ramoops, so a region written by either kernel
reads in the other.
.El
.Pp
Each size is rounded down to a power of two.
Each zone has Linux's header of 12 bytes: the signature 0x43474244, the
offset of the next byte to write and the bytes held, each 32 bits.
.Pp
A panic writes the end of the log into the next dmesg zone, once, after
its own record: Linux's header line,
.Ql ==== Ns Ar SECONDS . Ns Ar MICROSECONDS Ns Ql -D ,
the line
.Ql Panic#1 Part1 ,
then as many of the newest whole records of the log as fit.
The console zone takes every record of the log as it is made, from the
first, wrapping when full.
.Pp
At boot, a dmesg zone that holds such a header becomes the file
.Pa dmesg-ramoops- Ns Ar N ,
and the console zone, if it holds anything, the file
.Pa console-ramoops-0 ,
with the last boot's log; the console zone is then emptied for this
boot's log, as on Linux.
A record with parity ends with Linux's notice of what the parity mended:
.Ql ECC: No errors detected ,
or
.Ql ECC: Ar N Corrected bytes, Ar M unrecoverable blocks .
.Pp
Removing a file erases its zone, so the next boot does not show the
record again.
A dmesg zone not removed keeps its record across boots until a later
panic writes over it, the panics taking the zones in turn from the first.
Removing
.Pa console-ramoops-0
empties the console zone, this boot's log in it too, as on Linux.
.Pp
The root directory is root's with mode 0750, and each file root's with
mode 0444, as Linux makes them.
A file's size is its record's.
Its access time is the time of the first mount, when the file is made, as
Linux makes the files at mount; its modification and change times are the
time in its record's header, or the time of the first mount for the
console's, which has none.
Nothing else makes, writes or renames a file.
.Xr statfs 2
reports
.Dv PSTOREFS_MAGIC ,
0x6165676c.
The type is
.Ql pstore
to
.Xr mount 2 ,
which shows the same files at every mount.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /sys/fs/pstore/dmesg-ramoops- Ns Ar N
The end of the log at a panic, from dmesg zone
.Ar N .
.It Pa /sys/fs/pstore/console-ramoops-0
The last boot's log.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
On a machine with 4 GiB of RAM, a megabyte at 1 GiB, with records of 64 KiB
and parity:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ramoops.mem_address=0x40000000 ramoops.mem_size=0x100000
ramoops.record_size=0x10000 ramoops.console_size=0x10000 ramoops.ecc=1
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mount 2 ,
.Xr watchdog 4 ,
.Xr debugfs 5 ,
.Xr sysfs 5
.Sh BUGS
The kernel has no oops that it survives, so only a panic writes a dmesg
record; there is no ftrace or pmsg record, and no compression.
